'''Lemnoideae''' is a [[subfamily]] of flowering [[aquatic plant]]s, known as '''duckweeds''', '''water lentils''', or '''water''' '''lenses'''. They float on or just beneath the surface of still or slow-moving bodies of [[fresh water]] and [[wetlands]]. Also known as '''bayroot''', they arose from within the arum or aroid family ([[Araceae]]),<ref name="tam">{{cite journal |author1=Sheh-May Tam |author2=Peter C. Boyce |author3=Tim M. Upson |author4=Denis Barabé |author5=Anne Bruneau |author6=Felix Forest |author7=John S. Parker | year = 2004 | title = Intergeneric and infrafamilial phylogeny of subfamily Monsteroideae (Araceae) revealed by chloroplast <011>trnL-F sequences | journal = American Journal of Botany| volume = 91 | issue = 3 | pages = 490–498 | doi = 10.3732/ajb.91.3.490 | doi-access=free | pmid=21653404}}</ref> so often are classified as the subfamily Lemnoideae within the family Araceae. Other classifications, particularly those created prior to the end of the twentieth century, place them as a separate family, '''Lemnaceae'''.

These plants have a simple structure, lacking an obvious [[Stem (botany)|stem]] or [[Leaf|leaves]]. The greater part of each plant is a small organized "[[thallus]]" or "[[frond]]" structure only a few cells thick, often with air pockets ([[aerenchyma]]) that allow it to float on or just under the water surface. Depending on the species, each plant may have no root or may have one or more simple rootlets.<ref>{{cite book |last=Sculthorpe |first=Cyril Duncan |year=1985 |title=The Biology of Aquatic Vascular Plants |publisher=Koeltz Scientific Books|isbn=978-3-87429-257-3 |url=https://archive.org/details/biologyofaquatic0000scul |url-access=registration}}</ref>

Reproduction is mostly by [[asexual reproduction|asexual]] [[budding]] ([[vegetative reproduction]]), which occurs from a [[meristem]] enclosed at the base of the frond. Occasionally, three tiny "flowers" consisting of two [[stamens]] and a [[pistil]] are produced, by which [[sexual reproduction]] occurs. Some view this "flower" as a [[pseudanthium]], or reduced [[inflorescence]], with three flowers that are distinctly either female or male and which are derived from the [[spadix (botany)|spadix]] in the Araceae. Evolution of the duckweed inflorescence remains ambiguous due to the considerable evolutionary reduction of these plants from their earlier relatives.

The flower of the duckweed genus ''[[Wolffia]]'' is the smallest known, measuring merely 0.3&nbsp;mm long.<ref>{{cite book|last=Landolt|first=Elias|title=Biosystematic investigations in the family of duckweeds (Lemnaceae) Vol. 2: The family of Lemnaceae: a monographic study. – Morphology, karyology, ecology, geographic distribution, nomenclature, descriptions|year=1986|publisher=Eidgenössische Technische Hochschule Zürich|location=Zürich}}</ref> The fruit produced through this occasional reproduction is a [[utricle (fruit)|utricle]], and a [[seed]] is produced in a bag containing air that facilitates flotation.

== Duckweed in natural environments ==
One of the more important factors influencing the distribution of wetland plants, and aquatic plants in particular, is nutrient availability.<ref>{{cite book |last=Keddy |first=Paul A. |year=2010 |chapter=Fertility |title=Wetland Ecology: Principles and conservation |edition=2nd |publisher=Cambridge University Press |isbn=978-0-521-73967-2 |page=79 |chapter-url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eVeaSqFy2VgC&pg=PA79 |access-date=7 May 2012}}</ref> Duckweeds tend to be associated with fertile, even [[Eutrophication|eutrophic]] conditions. They can be spread by sticking to the feathers of waterfowl and the skin or fur of other amphibious animals, and thus transported inadvertently to new bodies of water.<ref>{{cite book |last=Hutchinson |first=G. Evelyn |year=1975 |title=A Treatise on Limnology |volume=3: Limnological botany |publisher=John Wiley & Sons |location=New York, NY}}</ref> The plants may also be transported by flooding or other moving water. In water bodies with constant currents or overflow, the plants are carried down the  channels and do not typically proliferate greatly, as they prefer still waters. However, in some locations, a cyclical pattern exists, driven by weather patterns, in which the plants proliferate greatly during periods of low water movement before they are carried away as rainy periods ensue.

Duckweed is an important, high-[[Protein (nutrient)|protein]] food source for [[waterfowl]] and many species of [[fish]].{{citation needed|date=September 2025}} The tiny plants provide cover for the vulnerable [[Fry (biology)|fry]] and [[tadpole]]s of many fishes and [[amphibian]]s. The plants are used as shelter by pond species, such as [[American bullfrog|bullfrogs]] and [[newt]]s, and fish such as [[bluegill]]s. They also provide shade and, although frequently confused with them, can reduce certain light-generated growths of [[photoautotrophic]] [[algae]].

== Use as human food crop ==
Duckweed is consumed in [[Laos]], [[Thailand]], and [[Myanmar]].<ref name="fc">{{cite journal |last1=Appenroth |first1=K.J. |last2=Sree |first2=K.S. |last3=Böhm |first3=V. |last4=Hammann |first4=S. |last5=Vetter |first5=W. |last6=Leiterer |first6=M. |last7=Jahreis |first7=G. |year=2017 |title=Nutritional value of duckweeds (''Lemnaceae'') as human food |journal=Food Chemistry |volume=217 |pages=266–273 |doi=10.1016/j.foodchem.2016.08.116 |pmid=27664634| s2cid=6617534 }}</ref> It produces more protein per square meter than [[soybean]]s, contains multiple nutrients, and can be substituted for salads or typical vegetables, making it a possible food source for humans.<ref name=fc/><ref name="dutch">{{cite journal |last1=de Beukelaar |first1=Myrthe F. |last2=Zeinstra |first2=Gertrude G. |last3=Mes |first3=Jurriaan J. |last4=Fischer |first4=Arnout R.H. |year=2019 |title=Duckweed as human food: The influence of meal context and information on duckweed acceptability of Dutch consumers |journal=Food Quality and Preference |volume=71 |issue=1 |pages=76–86 |doi=10.1016/j.foodqual.2018.06.005 |doi-access=free|url= https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950329318302295}}</ref><ref name="charm">{{cite web |title=Duckweed for food|publisher=European Duckweed Network |date=5 May 2025 |url=https://thecharmofduckweed.org/Food|access-date=18 August 2026}}</ref> It is cultivated as a vegetable in [[Israel]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Lemna, water lentils, duckweed |publisher=Plants for a Future, Plant Database |url=https://pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Lemna |access-date=2023-01-04|date=2026}}</ref>

[[NASA]]'s [[Caves of Mars Project]] identified duckweed as a candidate for Martian food production projects.<ref>{{cite web|date=2004 |title=Flat crops for Mars |website=The Caves of Mars |url=http://www.highmars.org/niac/niac04.html |access-date=11 January 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070701234427/http://www.highmars.org/niac/niac04.html |archive-date=1 July 2007}}</ref>

==Invasive species==
Despite some of these benefits, because duckweed thrives in high-nutrient wetland environments, the plants can be seen as a nuisance species when conditions favor excessive proliferation in environments that are traditionally low in nutrients or [[oligotrophic]].

One example of this problem occurs within the [[Everglades]], a mostly oligotrophic environment, when excess chemicals (that include fertilizers) are carried by storm runoff, or [[surface runoff]], into its waterways.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Cunningham |first1=William |last2=Cunningham |first2=Mary |year=2015 |title=Environmental Science: A global concern |edition=13th |publisher=McGraw-Hill Education |location=New York, NY |isbn=978-1-259-25572-4 |page=415}}</ref>

[[Urban runoff]] and [[agricultural pollution]] then begin to introduce increased levels of nutrients into the surrounding wetlands and waterways, which can cause a disruption to native ecology. These conditions allow for the invasion of a fast growing species such as duckweed to establish themselves, spread, and displace other native species such as [[Cladium|sawgrass]], and over time, result in widespread changes to the ecology of native sawgrass and [[sloughs|slough]] habitats within the Everglades.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Finkl |first1=Charles |last2=Makowski |first2=Christopher |year=2017 |title=Coastal Wetlands: Alteration and remediation |publisher=Springer |place=Cham, Switzerland |isbn=978-3-319-56178-3 |page=16 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=YG0tDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA3 |access-date=1 January 2021}}</ref>

== Taxonomy ==
[[File:Lemnoideae Systematics & Biology.jpg|thumb|right|Duckweeds belong to the order Alismatales and the family Araceae. (a)&nbsp;is a phylogenetic tree based on ribulose-1, 5-bisphosphate carboxylase large-subunit genes. (b)&nbsp;is a schematic ventral view of ''Spirodela'', to show the clonal, vegetative propagation of duckweeds. Daughter fronds&nbsp;(F1) originate from the vegetative node&nbsp;(No), from the mother frond F0 and remain attached to it by the stipule&nbsp;(Sti), which eventually breaks off, thereby releasing a new plant cluster. Daughter fronds may already initiate new fronds&nbsp;(F2) themselves before full maturity. Roots are attached at the prophyllum&nbsp;(P). (c)&nbsp;shows the progressive reduction from a leaf-like body with several veins and unbranched roots to a thallus-like morphology in the Lemnoideae.]]
The duckweeds have long been a taxonomic mystery, and usually have been considered to be their own family, the Lemnaceae. They primarily reproduce asexually. Flowers, if present at all, are small. Roots are either very much reduced, or absent entirely. They were suspected of being related to the Araceae as long ago as 1876, but until the advent of [[molecular phylogeny]], testing this hypothesis was difficult.

Starting in 1995, studies began to confirm their placement in the Araceae and since then, most systematists have considered them to be part of that family,<ref name=cabrera/> although in 2021, recognition again as a separate family was proposed.<ref name=AcosAppeBoriEdel21>{{Cite journal |last1=Acosta |first1=Kenneth |last2=Appenroth |first2=Klaus J. |last3=Borisjuk |first3=Ljudmilla |last4=Edelman |first4=Marvin |last5=Heinig |first5=Uwe |last6=Jansen |first6=Marcel A. K. |last7=Tokitaka |first7=Oyama |last8=Pasaribu |first8=Buntora |last9=Schubert |first9=Ingo |last10=Sorrels |first10=Shawn |last11=Sree |first11=K. Sowjanya |last12=Xu |first12=Shuqing |last13=Michael |first13=Todd P. |last14=Lam |first14=Eric |date=2021 |title=Return of the Lemnaceae: duckweed as a model plant system in the genomics and postgenomics era |journal=The Plant Cell |volume=33 |issue=10 |pages=3207–3234 |doi=10.1093/plcell/koab189 |pmid=34273173 |pmc=8505876 }}</ref>

