To identify different duckweed genomes, a DNA-based molecular identification system was developed based on seven [[plastid]]-markers proposed by the [[Consortium for the Barcode of Life]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Hollingsworth |first1=P.M. |display-authors=etal  |date=July 2009 |title=A DNA barcode for land plants |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |volume=106 |issue=31 |pages=12794–12797 |doi=10.1073/pnas.0905845106 |pmid=19666622 |pmc=2722355 |doi-access=free |url=http://xa.yimg.com/kq/groups/18463231/1250370658/name/A+DNA+barcode+for+land+plants.pdf |access-date=2 August 2012}}</ref> The ''atpF-atpH'' non-coding [[Spacer DNA|spacer]] was chosen as a universal [[DNA barcoding]] marker for species-level identification of duckweeds.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Wang|first=Wenqin|author2=Wu, Yongrui |author3=Yan, Yiheng |author4=Ermakova, Marina |author5=Kerstetter, Randall |author6= Messing, Joachim |year=2010 |title=DNA barcoding of the ''Lemnaceae'', a family of aquatic monocots |journal=BMC Plant Biology |volume=10 |issue=1 |page=205 |doi=10.1186/1471-2229-10-205 |pmid=20846439 |pmc=2956554 |bibcode=2010BMCPB..10..205W |doi-access=free }}</ref>

==Fossil record==
Extinct free-floating aquatic plants and [[palynology|pollen]] with affinities to the Lemnoideae first appear in the [[fossil]] record during the [[Late Cretaceous]] ([[Maastrichtian]]) as evidenced by floating leaves described as ''[[Aquaephyllum auriculatum]]'' from [[Patagonia]], [[Argentina]], and the lemnoid pollen [[genus]] ''[[Pandaniidites]]''.<ref name=Gallego>{{cite journal |author1=Gallego, J. |author2=Gandolfo, M.A. |author3=Cúneo, N.R. |author4=Zamaloa, M.C. |year=2014 |title=Fossil Araceae from the upper Cretaceous of Patagonia, Argentina, with implications on the origin of free-floating aquatic aroids |journal=Review of Palaeobotany and Palynology|volume=211 |pages=78–86 |doi=10.1016/j.revpalbo.2014.08.017 |bibcode=2014RPaPa.211...78G |hdl=11336/36506 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

Fossils of floating leaves with rootlets from the [[Paleocene]] of southern [[Saskatchewan]], [[Canada]], that were originally described as ''Lemna'' (''Spirodela'') ''scutata'' by [[John William Dawson]] in 1885, have been redescribed as ''[[Limnobiophyllum]]''.<ref name=Kvacek>{{cite journal |author=Kvaček, Z. |year=1995 |title=''Limnobiophyllum'' {{small|(Krassilov)}} – A fossil link between Araceae and Lemnaceae |journal=Aquatic Botany |volume=50 |pages=49–61|doi=10.1016/0304-3770(94)00442-O }}</ref> In addition to western North America, ''Limnobiophyllum'' has been reported from the Paleocene of eastern Russia and the [[Miocene]] of the [[Czech Republic]].<ref name=Kvacek /> Unusually complete specimens from the Paleocene of [[Alberta]], Canada, range from single leaves up to about {{cvt|4|cm|in}} in diameter to rosettes of up to four leaves, some of which were connected to adjacent plants by [[stolon]]s, and a few of which bear remains of [[flower]]s with [[anther]]s that contain ''Pandaniidites'' pollen.<ref name=Stockey>{{cite journal |author1=Stockey, R.A. |author2=Hoffman, G.L. |author3=Rothwell, G.W. |year=1997 |title=The fossil monocot ''Limnobiophyllum scutatum'': Resolving the phylogeny of Lemnaceae |journal=[[American Journal of Botany]] |volume=84 |issue=3 |pages=355–368|doi=10.2307/2446009 |jstor=2446009 |pmid=21708589 |s2cid=20957240 |doi-access=free }}</ref> Occurrences of lemnoid seeds described as ''[[Lemnospermum]]'' have also been reported.<ref name=Kvacek />

== Research and applications ==
Research and applications of duckweeds are promoted by two international organizations, The International Lemna Association<ref>{{cite web |title=The International Lemna Association (ILA) |type=official website |url=http://www.internationallemnaassociation.org/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121130023705/http://www.internationallemnaassociation.org/|url-status=usurped|archive-date=30 November 2012}}</ref> and the International Steering Committee on Duckweed Research and Applications.<ref>{{cite web |title=International Steering Committee on Duckweed Research and Applications (ISCDRA) |type=official website |url=http://lemnapedia.org/wiki/ISCDRA}}</ref>

In July 2008, the [[U.S. Department of Energy]] (DOE) [[Joint Genome Institute]] announced that the Community Sequencing Program would fund sequencing of the genome of the giant duckweed, ''[[Spirodela polyrhiza]]''. This was a priority project for DOE in 2009. The research was intended to facilitate new [[biomass]] and [[bioenergy]] programs.<ref>{{cite web |title=Duckweed genome sequencing has global implications |date=8 July 2008 |website=E! Science News (Esciencenews.com) |url=http://esciencenews.com/articles/2008/07/08/duckweed.genome.sequencing.has.global.implications |access-date=13 November 2011 |archive-date=24 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110524015107/http://esciencenews.com/articles/2008/07/08/duckweed.genome.sequencing.has.global.implications }}</ref> The results were published in February 2014. They provide insight into how this plant is adapted to rapid growth and an aquatic lifestyle.<ref>{{cite journal |author1=Wang, W. |display-authors=etal |year=2014 |title=The ''Spirodela polyrhiza'' genome reveals insights into its neotenous reduction fast growth and aquatic lifestyle |journal=[[Nature Communications]] |volume=5 |article-number=3311 |doi=10.1038/ncomms4311 |pmid=24548928 |pmc=3948053 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2014NatCo...5.3311W }}</ref>

===Potential clean energy source===
Duckweed is being studied by researchers around the world as a possible source of clean energy. In the U.S., in addition to being the subject of study by the DOE, both [[Rutgers University]] and [[North Carolina State University]] have ongoing projects to determine whether duckweed might be a source of cost-effective, clean, [[renewable energy]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Michael|first=Todd P. |year=2008 |title=Genome sequencing of the duckweed ''Spirodela polyrhiza'': A biofuels, bioremediation, and carbon cycling crop |publisher=[[Rutgers University]] |url=http://lemna.rutgers.edu/Michael_duckweed_JGIDOE_2008_final.pdf |access-date=7 May 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111003092955/http://lemna.rutgers.edu/Michael_duckweed_JGIDOE_2008_final.pdf|archive-date=3 October 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ncsu.edu/research/results/vol8n2/09.html |title=Researchers Find Fuel in Odd Places |publisher=Ncsu.edu |access-date=13 November 2011}}</ref> Duckweed is a good candidate as a [[biofuel]] because it grows rapidly, produces five to six times as much [[starch]] as corn per unit of area, and does not contribute to [[global warming]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Sims |first=Bryan |date=n.d. |title=Duckweed quacks volumes of potential |website=Biomassmagazine.com |url=http://www.biomassmagazine.com/article.jsp?article_id=2714&q=&page=all |access-date=13 November 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=Duckweed a possible solution to energy needs, researchers say |publisher=Pressofatlanticcity.com |date=3 May 2010 |url=http://www.pressofatlanticcity.com/news/press/cumberland/article_4ae59e12-56f1-11df-a5d8-001cc4c002e0.html |access-date=13 November 2011}}</ref> The rapid nature of duckweed has shown that it can double biomass within four and a half days.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Faizal |first1=Ahmad |last2=Sembada |first2=Anca Awal |last3=Priharto |first3=Neil |date=2021-01-01 |title=Production of bioethanol from four species of duckweeds (Landoltia punctata, Lemna aequinoctialis, Spirodela polyrrhiza, and Wolffia arrhiza) through optimization of saccharification process and fermentation with Saccharomyces cerevisiae |url= |journal=Saudi Journal of Biological Sciences |volume=28 |issue=1 |pages=294–301 |doi=10.1016/j.sjbs.2020.10.002 |issn=1319-562X |pmc=7785427 |pmid=33424309|bibcode=2021SJBS...28..294F }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ziegler |first1=P. |last2=Adelmann |first2=K. |last3=Zimmer |first3=S. |last4=Schmidt |first4=C. |last5=Appenroth |first5=K.-J. |date=2014-05-06 |editor-last=Keurentjes |editor-first=J. |title=Relative in vitro growth rates of duckweeds (L emnaceae) – the most rapidly growing higher plants |url=|journal=Plant Biology |language=en |volume=17 |issue=s1 |pages=33–41 |doi=10.1111/plb.12184 |pmid=24803032 |issn=1435-8603}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Guo |first1=Ling |last2=Fang |first2=Yang |last3=Jin |first3=Yanling |last4=He |first4=Kaize |last5=Zhao |first5=Hai |date=2023-11-01 |title=High starch duckweed biomass production and its highly-efficient conversion to bioethanol |url= |journal=Environmental Technology & Innovation |volume=32 |article-number=103296 |doi=10.1016/j.eti.2023.103296 |issn=2352-1864|doi-access=free |bibcode=2023EnvTI..3203296G }}</ref> Duckweed removes carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, and it may have value for [[climate change mitigation]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Carbon Neutral Energy |website=American Energy Independence |url=http://www.americanenergyindependence.com/carbonneutral.aspx |access-date=13 November 2011 |archive-date=27 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110927223526/http://www.americanenergyindependence.com/carbonneutral.aspx }}</ref>

===Filtration of contaminants and nutrients===
The plants can provide [[nitrate]] removal, if cropped, and the duckweeds are important in the process of [[bioremediation]] because they grow rapidly, absorbing excess mineral nutrients, particularly [[nitrogen]] and [[phosphates]]. For these reasons, they are touted as water purifiers of untapped value.<ref>{{cite web |title=Duckweed wastewater treatment and reuse for fodder (West Bank) |website=Idrc.ca |url=http://www.idrc.ca/es/ev-6314-201-1-DO_TOPIC.html |access-date=13 November 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110608214049/http://www.idrc.ca/es/ev-6314-201-1-DO_TOPIC.html |archive-date=8 June 2011  }}</ref>

The Swiss Department of Water and Sanitation in Developing Countries, associated with the Swiss Federal Institute for Environmental Science and Technology, asserts that as well as the food and agricultural values, duckweed also may be used for [[wastewater treatment]] to capture toxins and for odor control, and that if a mat of duckweed is maintained during harvesting for removal of the toxins captured thereby, it prevents the development of algae and controls the breeding of [[mosquitoes]].<ref>{{cite journal |last=Iqbal |first=Sascha |date=March 1999 |title=Duckweed aquaculture: Potentials, possibilities, and limitations for combined wastewater treatment and animal feed production in developing countries |journal=SANDEC Report |volume=6 |issue=99 |url=http://www.eawag.ch/forschung/sandec/publikationen/wra/dl/duckweed.pdf |access-date=31 January 2012}}</ref> The same publication provides an extensive list of references for many duckweed-related topics.

These plants also may play a role in conservation of water because a cover of duckweed will reduce [[evaporation]] of water when compared to the rate of a similarly sized water body with a clear surface.

Duckweed also functions as a bioremediator by effectively filtering contaminants such as bacteria, nitrogen, phosphates, and other nutrients from naturally occurring bodies of water, constructed wetlands, and wastewater.<ref>{{cite press release |title=Duckweed genome sequencing has global implications. Pond scum can undo pollution, fight global warming and alleviate world hunger |publisher=[[Rutgers University]] |date=8 July 2008 |url=https://www.rutgers.edu/news/duckweed-genome-sequencing-has-global-implications |access-date=10 March 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |author=Cross, John W. |title=Practical duckweed: Application areas and sponsors |website=Mobot.org |url=http://www.mobot.org/jwcross/duckweed/practical_duckweed.htm |access-date=13 November 2011 |archive-date=25 September 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160925121914/http://www.mobot.org/jwcross/duckweed/practical_duckweed.htm }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Knibb |first=Wayne |date=July 2001 – June 2004 |title=Bioremediation of aquaculture waste and degraded waterways using finfish |publisher=Queensland Government |place=Australia |series=Department of Primary Industries & Fisheries Tools |url=http://www2.dpi.qld.gov.au/far/9212.html|access-date=6 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071020015703/http://www2.dpi.qld.gov.au/far/9212.html |archive-date=20 October 2007}}</ref>

