A start-up, microTERRA, based in Mexico has attempted to use duckweed to clean the water in privately owned [[aquaculture]] farms. The plants use nitrogen and phosphorus produced from fish waste as fertilizer, while simultaneously cleaning the water as it grows. The water can then be reused by the [[aquaculture]] farmers, and the duckweed, which has a 35-42% protein content, can be harvested as a source of sustainable plant-based [[Protein (nutrient)#Sources|protein]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=3 Eco-Innovators Tackling Pollution and Climate Change|url=https://magazine.columbia.edu/article/3-eco-innovators-tackling-pollution-and-climate-change|access-date=2021-11-24|website=Columbia Magazine|language=en}}</ref>

